\begin{document}

    2025 is arguably one of the most significant turning points in American politics and 

\vspace{10pt}

    culture. Media has been filled up with families being torn apart, neighbors being detained, and 

\vspace{10pt}

    friends being deported. Americans are divided on the matter—some argue that the “mass 

\vspace{10pt}

    deportations” violate human rights, while others claim the actions are necessary for national 

\vspace{10pt}

\begin{figure}[h]

\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{/convert/ASumFOT/localstore/2025-immigration-essayassets/0.jpg}

\centering

\end{figure}

    The question should not be whether immigration is good or bad; it should be, “How can 

\vspace{10pt}

    immigration uphold American values?” The Constitution states, ““We the People of the United 

\vspace{10pt}

    States, in Order to form a more perfect Union, establish Justice, insure domestic Tranquility, 

\vspace{10pt}

    provide for the common defense, promote the general Welfare, and secure the Blessings of 

\vspace{10pt}

    Liberty to ourselves and our Posterity, do ordain and establish this Constitution for the United 

\vspace{10pt}

    States of America.” Like the rest of American legislation, immigration policies should promote 

\vspace{10pt}

    American wealth, safety, and sovereignty. 

\vspace{10pt}

    Since the 1980s, illegal immigration has grown as an issue. Respectively in 2012 and 

\vspace{10pt}

    2014, President Obama introduced Deferred Action for Childhood Arrivals (DACA) and 

\vspace{10pt}

    Deferred Action for Parents of Americans (DAPA) to prevent the separation of undocumented 

\vspace{10pt}

    immigrants and their American-born children [1]. Conversely, in 2016, President Trump 

\vspace{10pt}

    tightened immigration measures. In addition to building a border wall, Title 42 expelled 400,000 

\vspace{10pt}

    unauthorized migrants, and the “zero tolerance policy” separated 3,900 children from their 

\vspace{10pt}

    undocumented parents [2]. By 2019, the number of illegal immigrants in America had dropped to 

\vspace{10pt}

    a decade-low of 10.2 million people [3]. In 2021, President Biden halted the border wall’s 

\vspace{10pt}

    construction and encouraged family reunification for undocumented immigrants. As an 

\vspace{10pt}

    alternative to immigration detention, Biden’s “catch and release” policy released migrants 

\vspace{10pt}

    awaiting immigration status into public communities. This caused a first-time rise in illegal 

\vspace{10pt}

    immigration, reaching 11 million illegal immigrants in the U.S. by 2022 [4]. In 2025, the Trump 

\vspace{10pt}

\par

\vspace{10pt}

    administration pulled the “catch and release” policy, declared a national emergency at the border, 

\vspace{10pt}

    and now plans to complete the border wall [5]. All these swinging policies cause one to ask, 

\vspace{10pt}

    “How have recent immigration policies contributed to American values?” 

\vspace{10pt}

    As stated in the Constitution, enriching American wealth is one goal of immigration. In 

\vspace{10pt}

    2009, illegal households used \$28.8 billion in government resources, while contributing \$9.5 

\vspace{10pt}

    billion in taxes [6]. Furthermore, illegal immigration often results in lower wages for legal 

\vspace{10pt}

    workers. In 2019, undocumented workers earned 42\% less than citizens and legal immigrants 

\vspace{10pt}

    [7]. When employers hire illegal workers, it lowers wages and working conditions for legal 

\vspace{10pt}

    residents. When illegal workers do not pay taxes, a lowered federal tax income burdens 

\vspace{10pt}

\begin{figure}[h]

\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{/convert/ASumFOT/localstore/2025-immigration-essayassets/1.jpg}

\centering

\end{figure}

    New immigrants should fill both high and low skill labor gaps without harming the 

\vspace{10pt}

    American public. In the early 1900s, immigrants entering Ellis Island were required to have at 

\vspace{10pt}

    least \$25.00 to ensure that they would not immediately go on welfare. Similarly, the U.S. should 

\vspace{10pt}

    prioritize high-skilled workers who are not only self-sufficient, but also offer knowledge, skills, 

\vspace{10pt}

    and innovation. Yet, America still needs low-skilled labor— although this may decrease with the 

\vspace{10pt}

    development of AI and the robot industry. To prevent deteriorating conditions and wages, the 

\vspace{10pt}

    U.S. could implement a minimum wage. This will balance competition between native and 

\vspace{10pt}

    foreign workers while protecting the rights of parties. 

\vspace{10pt}

    Public safety is another priority of American immigration. The Constitution mandates 

\vspace{10pt}

    that legislation “insure domestic Tranquility” and “provide for the common defence.” Criminal 

\vspace{10pt}

    convictions and immigration violations doubled from fiscal years 2021 to 2023 [8]. In 1998, 63\% 

\vspace{10pt}

    of all federal arrests were of U.S. citizens; in 2018, 64\% of all federal arrests were of non-U.S. 

\vspace{10pt}

    citizens [9]. These significant shifts undermine American safety and security. 

\vspace{10pt}

\par

\vspace{10pt}

    While migrants will always seek illegal ways to enter our nation, securing the border is 

\vspace{10pt}

    the first step. Texas’s Operation Lone Star highlights the effectiveness of stronger border 

\vspace{10pt}

    security. Since 2021, the program has resulted in an 86\% decrease in illegal crossings [10]. 

\vspace{10pt}

    Combined with deploying AI technologies such as drones and robots, the border wall can be 

\vspace{10pt}

    transformed into a “‘smart” wall, ensuring the safety of the American people on a higher level. 

\vspace{10pt}

    Above all, immigration must be conducted under American sovereignty. Welcoming 

\vspace{10pt}

    illegal immigrants is simply unfair to those immigrating the legal way. Since 2021, Customs and 

\vspace{10pt}

    Border Protection has recorded over 10.8 million encounters with illegal immigrants [11]. Recent 

\vspace{10pt}

    policies not only were weak on these threats, but supported them by offering benefits to 

\vspace{10pt}

    lawbreakers. In 2022, 4.4 million U.S.-born children lived with illegal immigrant parents [12]. 

\vspace{10pt}

    This is a clear example of how loopholes in U.S. immigration law have been exploited. 

\vspace{10pt}

    Compassion must be shown towards the millions of families who have suffered because of an 

\vspace{10pt}

    unclear and inaccessible immigration system that encouraged them to risk illegal immigration. 

\vspace{10pt}

    Yet, this is precisely the reason why immigration policies need to change; more suffering must be 

\vspace{10pt}

    prevented in the future. The rule of law of the people cannot bend to an individual's emotions. 

\vspace{10pt}

    Thus, America should establish clear consequences of breaking the law. 

\vspace{10pt}

    Ultimately, the U.S. can encourage legal over illegal entry by increasing more lawful 

\vspace{10pt}

    opportunities. For example, a point-based immigration system would acknowledge family 

\vspace{10pt}

    relations, employment, and humanitarian aid as important factors to one’s entry. A transparent 

\vspace{10pt}

    and accessible point-based approach will encourage more immigrants with valuable 

\vspace{10pt}

    contributions to come to America. This system has already been adopted by Canada, the United 

\vspace{10pt}

    Kingdom, and Australia. Fulfilling the Constitutional values, a point-based system would 

\vspace{10pt}

    “establish Justice” in the American immigration system and relieve hardships for those coming 

\vspace{10pt}

    to our nation in the future. 

\vspace{10pt}

\par

\vspace{10pt}

    Immigration has been a debated issue from the birth of our nation through the Civil War, 

\vspace{10pt}

    the World Wars, and the post-war era. As this discussion will inevitably go on, we must be 

\vspace{10pt}

    grounded by the values of America as stated in the Constitution. Let us welcome new immigrants 

\vspace{10pt}

    to keep America “‘a city upon a hill”, promoting wealth, security, and sovereignty for both itself 

\vspace{10pt}

    and the world. 

\vspace{10pt}

\par

\vspace{10pt}

